EXERCISE 3
Visualize sales data in Excel using bar, line, and pie charts and interpret patterns
Objective
To analyze data using visual representations.
Procedure
Steps in Excel for Bar Chart – Sales by Product
Arrange data like this:
Product | Sales
Select the Product and Sales columns.
Go to Insert → Charts → Bar Chart → Clustered Bar.
Add:
Chart Title: Sales by Product
Data Labels (optional for clarity)
Interpretation
Products with higher bars indicate stronger sales performance.
A large gap between bars suggests uneven product demand.
Products with low sales may need marketing focus or redesign.
Steps in Excel for Line Chart – Regional  Sales
Arrange data like this:
Region | Sales
Select the Region and Sales columns.
Go to Insert → Charts → Line Chart → Line with Markers.
Add:
Chart Title: Monthly Sales Trend
Axis Titles (Month, Sales)
Steps in Excel for Pie Chart – Regional Sales Contribution
Arrange data like this:
Region | Sales
Select the Region and Sales columns.
Go to Insert → Charts → Pie Chart → 2-D Pie.
Add:
Chart Title: Regional Sales Contribution
Percentage Data Labels


Expected Output
• Bar, Line, and Pie charts









Overall Trend & Pattern Interpretation
Product Analysis: Certain products outperform others, indicating customer preference and potential focus areas.
Time-Based Trends: Monthly sales show seasonal patterns, with sales peaking during specific months.
Regional Performance: Sales are concentrated in a few regions, suggesting the need for diversification or targeted regional strategies.
Result
Sales data is visualized using bar, line, and pie charts, highlighting trends, regional contributions, and top-performing products.





